{
  "$comment": "Fingerprints of every sample in test-output/samples/ (written by the BUILT server, scripts/generate-samples.ts). Regenerate deliberately with `npx tsx scripts/verify-samples.ts --update`, and say why in the release notes: a changed hash means existing output changed. Mode `bytes` is a SHA-256 of the file; mode `semantic` is a SHA-256 of a canonical projection of the document, used for encrypted samples (CSPRNG file key and IVs) and signed samples (a per-revision /ID drawn at signing time), whose bytes can never repeat. Each entry's `since` names the release whose output the hash is: it is carried forward untouched while the sample is unchanged, and moves to the release doing the rebaseline when the hash changes, so the chain 1.7.0 -> 1.8.0 -> … stays auditable and every re-anchoring is visible in the diff.",
  "baselineVersion": "1.7.0",
  "provenance": "Chain origin: pdfnative-mcp 1.7.0 on pdfnative 1.8.0. Release 1.6.0 had no determinism plumbing (no pinned process instant, dates written in the host time zone by pdfnative 1.7), so there is no earlier reference to chain from and every entry is anchored to 1.7.0. Verification of this first baseline: the corpus was generated twice from the built server, once under TZ=UTC and once under TZ=Pacific/Kiritimati; 94 of 96 samples were byte-identical across the two runs. The two that moved are the ones fingerprinted semantically, by construction: the encrypt_pdf output (CSPRNG file key, salts and IVs, ISO 32000-1 7.6) and the signed corpus entry (a throwaway RSA key generated per run, never written to disk). The PDF/A-claiming files were accepted or rejected as expected by veraPDF 1.30.2 (27 PASS, 6 XFAIL) and the PDF/X-4 files by pdfnative validatePdfX() (4 PASS, 2 XFAIL) on the same bytes. From 1.8.0 on, an entry that still reads 1.7.0 is being held to the bytes 1.7.0 emitted; re-anchoring one is a declared output change (release notes, Upgrade section).",
  "creationDate": "2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z",
  "timezone": "UTC",
